WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.840
Good morning. Hank. It's Tuesday. 
I'm still on vacation, but don't worry I brought

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.200
35,000 sheets of paper with me.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.080
And as you can see here, I've signed very nearly 120,000 sheets.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.400
Today, I thought I'd answer some real questions from 
real Nerdfighters about signing.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.540
Beginning with: Are you literally signing 
200,000 sheets of paper?

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.200
Yes, so I sign all of these sheets.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.920
And then I send them to Pennsylvania

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.460
where they are bound one-by-one

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.720
into copies of my new book, 𝑇𝑢𝑟𝑡𝑙𝑒𝑠 𝐴𝑙𝑙 𝑡𝘩𝑒 𝑊𝑎𝑦 𝐷𝑜𝑤𝑛.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.280
And because the book obviously has to be printed somewhat in advance

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.720
of its publication on October 10th,

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.760
I have to finish signing by, like, August 17th.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.200
Are you aware that your signature is currently 
worth negative four dollars on Amazon?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.630
Yeah, I actually think it's negative three dollars 
at the moment,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:37.960
but it's true that the signed edition

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.420
is currently less expensive than the unsigned one.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.840
I get the same royalty regardless

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.940
and have no control over Amazon's discounting.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.780
But also Amazon might be onto something

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.080
because for like a year after 𝘛𝘩𝘦 𝘍𝘢𝘶𝘭𝘵 𝘪𝘯 𝘖𝘶𝘳 𝘚𝘵𝘢𝘳𝘴 came out,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.680
unsigned copies were much rarer than signed ones.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.780
How many paper cuts have you gotten so far?

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.149
Amazingly, none yet.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:56.260
But I spend a lot of time worrying about it.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.660
Why two hundred thousand?

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.020
Uh...at the time it seemed like enough, 
but not too much.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:01.900
Why u do dis?

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.460
To be honest, I'm not totally sure,

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.840
but I do have some theories.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.820
One reason is that it's always struck me as unfair

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.240
that people who don't live near major Metropolitan Areas

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.420
or for whatever reason can't go to author events

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.220
don't get signed books unless they pay a surcharge.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.960
But there are also a lot of selfish reasons,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.000
one of which is that you know like 
for the last 2000 days or so,

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.140
I've been trying to write this book, 
and on most of those days

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.951
I didn't know if what I was writing
would ever make it into the book.

00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:28.540
A lot of those days I couldn't even 
tell if the work I was doing

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.160
was getting me closer to a book I was proud of 
or further away from it.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.220
With this I know that I'm doing something people will like,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.420
and each time I do it,

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.460
I get a little closer to being done;

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.380
writing is not like that for me.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.400
Does your hand hurt?

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.540
No, I got some incredibly effective physical therapy

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.120
when I was doing this for 𝘛𝘩𝘦 𝘍𝘢𝘶𝘭𝘵 𝘪𝘯 𝘖𝘶𝘳 𝘚𝘵𝘢𝘳𝘴

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.680
because I have Ulnar Nerve Entrapment,

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.700
and since then I've been able to just sign all day.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.140
Why don't you hire someone to make a machine 
that perfectly mimics your signature?

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:52.860
You know there are some jobs

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.360
that robots just can't do, and 
one of them is monotonously

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.280
creating the same scratch on a page
two hundred thousand times.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.240
How consistent does your signature tend to be?

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.480
So I think it varies wildly,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.400
but Sarah thinks I just spend way too much time

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.280
looking at my signature.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.220
I've grabbed three here that I think are very different 
to give you a sense of the variation.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:11.000
How many papers can you sign in an hour?

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.740
It takes about two hours to do a thousand,

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:16.160
so in total this project is gonna to take 
around 400 hours.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.220
Would you please put that pen to work on another book

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.520
instead of a zillion fairly anonymous tokens?

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.080
No, see the problem is the pen does
not want to work on another book.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.620
What kind of pen are you using?

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.280
I use Sharpies and lots of them.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.260
I'm in something of a blue period by the way.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.560
Are you sponsored by Sharpie?

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.280
No, I wish!

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.220
I'm ready to sell out for some of that 
sweet sweet Sharpie money.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.600
But no, I buy my Sharpies.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.480
How do you decide what colors to sign in?

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.980
Well, you're deciding the color breakdown

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:39.840
at the last hundred thousand.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.580
Thanks to your votes

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.100
Do you ever write or draw random things 
in some of the books along with your signature?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.660
Yes, for reasons that will become obvious to

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.840
readers of the book, I sometimes make a spiral.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.100
What good media have you been 
enjoying while signing?

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.560
I'm mostly listening to podcasts including all of these,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.360
but I also listen to Pride and Prejudice on audiobook 
and it is still great

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.500
What's your rhythm? 
Pull-play-sign-pass?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.380
I guess my rhythm looks approximately like this, 
and then once that done pile gets big enough,

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.220
I stack them and start again.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.160
Can you draw turtles all the way down on mine?

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.640
No, but I did draw signatures 
all the way down on this one.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.100
What are some interesting thoughts 
you've had during this process?

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.869
I would actually say the chief pleasure of it

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.680
is that I don't have very many thoughts at all.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.459
When you first started signing the books, 
did you think it was fun?

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:19.049
I honestly still think it's fun.

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:20.540
Where can I buy the book?

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.640
Somebody knows how to ensure their 
question gets answered!

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.760
The signed edition has its own ISBN.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.600
There are links in the doobly-doo below.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.840
But also pretty much wherever books are sold.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:29.660
And lastly,

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.040
can I Hanklerfish some?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.520
Hank, I'll be putting this box in the mail shortly.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.800
I'll see you on Friday.

